WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:02.120
Stephen Hawking was 
one of the most

00:00:02.120 --> 00:00:04.560
influential scientists 
of his generation.

00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:07.560
From black holes to The Simpsons,

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:12.160
his impact on science and popular 
culture is hard to overestimate.

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:16.940
In this video we're going to look at just 
three publications that shaped his career.

00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:22.340
Number one: 'The singularities of
gravitational collapse and cosmology'

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:25.380
This paper was published in 1970.

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:32.060
It was a time when physicists were still finding some of the implications of general relativity hard to swallow,

00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:36.940
in particular the possibility of black holes 
and infinitely dense singularities.

00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:40.580
Hawking and others had already
 shown that singularities should form,

00:00:40.580 --> 00:00:44.040
at least in an abstract mathematical setting.

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:49.770
In fact, in his PhD thesis, Hawking had
explored how the universe itself

00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:54.360
might have originated from a
 singularity at the beginning of time.

00:00:54.360 --> 00:01:00.020
But in the 1970 paper, Hawking worked 
with eminent physicist Roger Penrose

00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:03.780
and showed that general relativity, 
even in the real world,

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:06.320
really does predict singularities.

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:09.680
Number two: 'Black hole explosions?'

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:15.820
As time went on Stephen Hawking's name would 
become inextricably linked with black holes.

00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:19.400
This paper really was called 'Black hole explosions?' 
with a question mark.

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:22.580
He had a knack for grabbing people's attention.

00:01:22.580 --> 00:01:26.790
It describes what came to be
known as Hawking radiation

00:01:26.790 --> 00:01:30.860
which many physicists 
regard as his most important legacy.

00:01:30.860 --> 00:01:35.540
Hawking was trying to reconcile two
seemingly incompatible theories.

00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:41.640
One was general relativity, which works well for 
describing the largest scales in the universe,

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:47.160
the other was quantum theory, which
describes the smallest scales.

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:50.360
Hawking realised that if you believe both theories,

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:54.420
the event horizon of a black hole should emit radiation.

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:58.940
He had shown that black holes are not actually black.

00:01:58.940 --> 00:02:04.700
To this day physicists continue to argue over 
the implications of Hawking radiation

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:09.580
and in particular about whether it erases
information from the universe,

00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:12.080
which would violate quantum theory.

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:16.480
And finally, number three: 'A brief history of time'.

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:20.660
This is not a scientific paper 
but rather a popular science book

00:02:20.660 --> 00:02:24.980
and it is this book which would catapult Hawking 
from prominent scientist

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:28.800
to probably the most famous scientist of his time.

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:35.400
It gave him a new platform as a science communicator, 
a spokesperson and a cultural phenomenon.

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:42.180
Ever since people around the world have been
fascinated by his story, his intellect and his tenacity.

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:46.860
Many now know Stephen Hawking 
for his characteristic synthetic voice,

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:49.380
his appearances on television shows

00:02:49.460 --> 00:02:52.880
or his controversial opinions
on a whole host of issues

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:55.500
from AI through to alien life.

00:02:55.960 --> 00:03:01.280
But behind the pop-culture icon, there
was a scientist whose legacy will endure.

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:04.000
And as Hawking himself once said:

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:09.620
"I hope I will be remembered for my work on black holes
and the origin of the Universe

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:13.160
not for things like appearing on The Simpsons."

